KG42.register("nodes-bebde227443147ca8c8a5398",{"id":"COMP-d90b7b5c0051250f","code":"CLR017","label":"Electricity allocation prioritizing favorable bids on the same market side","definition":"Determine the allocation order according to the role-related price advantage of orders on the same side, and allocate the declared electricity quantity according to this priority relationship.","layer":"L5","template_code":"DA","concept_id":"clearing.local","concept_ids":["clearing.local"],"identity_dimensions":{"definition":"Determine the allocation order according to the role-related price advantage of orders on the same side, and allocate the declared electricity quantity according to this priority relationship.","object":"The allocation order and transacted electricity quantity of energy buy and sell orders, retaining the different price roles of buyers and sellers.","inputs":"Order quotations, declared electricity quantities, trading roles, and the quantity conditions actually specified by the source.","operation":"On the trading side for which the source explicitly specifies ordering, the one with the stronger price competitive advantage is allocated first: buyers with higher payment quotations have priority, and sellers with lower asking prices have priority; electricity quantity is allocated according to this order.","outputs":"The allocated participants and transacted electricity quantities constrained by the price priority relationship; the specific quantity calculation rules are retained by the members.","boundary":"Limited to L5/DA allocation; it is not required that every member simultaneously specify two-sided ordering, equal-price handling, or a complete quantity algorithm; price compatibility checks, pricing, and payment cannot replace an explicit allocation priority relationship."},"exclusions":[["∀i∈{1,…,N_B}: 0≤B_i^{Qfill}≤B_i^Q; ∀j∈{1,…,N_S}: 0≤S_j^{Qfill}≤S_j^Q, with electricity quantity in kWh.","The explicit allocation priority relationship applies to sellers with different asking prices; no equal-price ordering rule is added.","The scenario is local P2P surplus solar energy trading and discriminatory k-DA.","The buyer-benefit winning conditions and the individual's strategic assumptions about other groups in the complete quotation are retained in their original context and are not extrapolated as a complete buyer allocation algorithm."],["A closed order book and a 15-minute discrete double auction are adopted; equal-price handling is not extrapolated.","x_i are, respectively, the consumer's maximum willingness-to-pay price and the prosumer's minimum selling price, in MU/kWh; y_i are, respectively, the requested or offered energy quantity, in kWh, and no complete numerical bounds are given.","min{y_1,y_24} and min{y_5,y_22} are only illustrative examples and are not generalized as a complete allocation algorithm for all orders.","Allocation upper and lower limits, near-real-time granularity, and adaptation to different market time horizons are general framework recommendations and are not rewritten as an implemented unified quantity constraint.","The branch in which consumers whose bids are not below the clearing price obtain additional energy through a similar auction or a preset price is retained separately.","The last allocated bid is used for uniform pricing, and the allocated participants are used for payment; both are subsequent uses of the allocation result and are not included in the parent operation."]],"members":[{"id":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"A buyer's requested energy quantity limits its cleared allocation: cleared kWh cannot exceed requested kWh. Scope: Per-buyer energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market. A seller's offered energy quantity limits its cleared allocation: cleared kWh cannot exceed supplied kWh. Scope: Per-seller energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market. A seller with a lower ask price clears its bid before sellers with higher ask prices. Scope: Seller-side clearing priority under the natural ordering rule in the studied discriminatory k-DA. Condition: When comparing sellers with different ask prices.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Per-buyer energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market. | Per-seller energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market. | Seller-side clearing priority under the natural ordering rule in the studied discriminatory k-DA.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12","v141-b-g01-ev-13","v141-b-g01-ev-18b","v141-b-g02-E07"]},"constraint_refs":["05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"850d886badc26673e6b7a831cc3bc57a6da0fb870f80bb116a79f99fe8b77d18","paper":"05-double_auction","rule_ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"05-double_auction::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"Submitted buy orders provide requested quantities and bid prices to local clearing, which allocates the highest bidder first and then lower bidders. Scope: BMG specified local double-auction mechanism Submitted sell orders provide offered quantities and prices to the market mechanism responsible for local energy allocation. Scope: BMG specified local double-auction mechanism Matching participants' buy and sell orders is specified as the means of achieving efficient local energy allocation. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4 Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price receive additional energy allocated through a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. Scope: BMG allocation of additional hyper-local or traditional energy sources to consumers Condition: Consumers do not undercut the clearing price. Minimum and maximum energy-allocation constraints should exist, especially to account for prosumer generation constraints. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4 Different market time horizons should ideally be implemented, and allocation mechanisms must be tailored to achieve efficient allocation at each stage. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design across electricity-market time horizons Local energy allocation should preferably operate at near-real-time granularity. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4 Local clearing uses a closed order book and a time-discrete double auction in 15-minute slots. Scope: BMG specified double-auction mechanism","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"BMG allocation of additional hyper-local or traditional energy sources to consumers | BMG specified double-auction mechanism | BMG specified local double-auction mechanism | General microgrid market-mechanism design across electricity-market time horizons | General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e35"]},"constraint_refs":["24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C007","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C008"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"1bbe8c3fe342874c6a3359fb3aba730cc3386f72459200ac03dd4f4c78b1f84b","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R010::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","market_meaning":"Local allocation determines the price-setting last allocated bid, whose price becomes the uniform market clearing price for that time slot. Scope: BMG closed-order-book double-auction design with 15 min time slots. Condition: When the described double-auction mechanism is used. A clearing price is determined in every time slot; prices vary with demand, supply and socio-economic characteristics. Scope: BMG stated time-slot pricing design. Condition: When the described time-slot pricing design is used. Energy surplus should lower the microgrid energy price. Scope: General microgrid-market pricing recommendations in the C1–C7 framework. Condition: There is an energy surplus. A lack of local energy increases the market price. Scope: General microgrid-market pricing discussion in the C1–C7 framework. Condition: Local energy is lacking. The local energy price may exceed the external grid price. Scope: General microgrid-market pricing discussion in the C1–C7 framework. Condition: Socio-economic reasons, such as supporting local renewable energy, are considered. The electricity price was predetermined and fixed at the traditional price for renewable energy in Brooklyn. Scope: BMG reported three month trial with one consumer and one prosumer. Condition: During this trial, in which the market mechanism was not actively used. Each transaction may have an individual transaction price. Scope: BMG proposed further market-mechanism trials, before selection of the final mechanism. Condition: If the proposed pay-as-you bid order book is used.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"BMG closed-order-book double-auction design with 15 min time slots. | BMG proposed further market-mechanism trials, before selection of the final mechanism. | BMG reported three month trial with one consumer and one prosumer. | BMG stated time-slot pricing design. | General microgrid-market pricing discussion in the C1–C7 framework. | General microgrid-market pricing recommendations in the C1–C7 framework.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e31","v141-b-g03-e010","v141-b-g03-e016"]},"constraint_refs":["24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C001"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"e5dc359e0f8848970cafe680480a92663cbc24c7dcee3b49ef9157fafdfb1042","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R010","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","market_meaning":"Local allocation supplies the allocated participants between whom financial transactions are carried out according to predefined payment rules. Scope: BMG virtual-market financial transactions between allocated participants. The local transaction price determined by the pricing mechanism is used for monetary payment; the illustrated BMG payment rules use the clearing price. Scope: Schematic local order-book transactions and the illustrated BMG payment rules. Payment is carried out once counterparty matching is completed. Scope: BMG smart-contract market transaction workflow. Condition: Matching has been completed. Consumers pay their producing neighbors for feeding renewable generation into the distribution grid. Scope: Physical-layer interpretation of BMG virtual-market payments. Condition: Producing neighbors feed renewable generation into the distribution grid.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"BMG smart-contract market transaction workflow. | BMG virtual-market financial transactions between allocated participants. | Physical-layer interpretation of BMG virtual-market payments. | Schematic local order-book transactions and the illustrated BMG payment rules.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g02-e30","v141-b-g02-e35","v141-b-g03-e022"]},"constraint_refs":["24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C005"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"cbcdd5e0a18793905b4452d98d282abe6114cf83dee8226cbc4b4d7be776a3c1","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false}],"paper_ids":["05-double_auction","24-brooklyn_microgrid"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-12","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2","anchor_text":"j re BiQfill Cleared kWh quantity for buyer i ; ≤BiQ Qfill Qm Sj Cleared kWh quantity for seller j ;","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 3","image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Where, $N_B$, $N_S$, $N_T$ Total number of buyers, sellers, and trades; $B_i^Q$ kWh quantity demanded by buyer i; $S_j^Q$ kWh quantity supplied by seller j; $B_i^{Qfill}$ Cleared kWh quantity for buyer i; $\\leq B_i^Q$; $S_j^{Qfill}$ Cleared kWh quantity for seller j; $\\leq S_j^Q$; $T_m^Q$ Total kWh quantity transacted for trade m.","paper":"05-double_auction","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Where, $N_B$, $N_S$, $N_T$ Total number of buyers, sellers, and trades; $B_i^Q$ kWh quantity demanded by buyer i; $S_j^Q$ kWh quantity supplied by seller j; $B_i^{Qfill}$ Cleared kWh quantity for buyer i; $\\leq B_i^Q$; $S_j^{Qfill}$ Cleared kWh quantity for seller j; $\\leq S_j^Q$; $T_m^Q$ Total kWh quantity transacted for trade m."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-13","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"3","anchor_text":"u ke In this study, a double auction is of interest so that both buyers and us sellers may submit bi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this study, a double auction is of interest so that both buyers and sellers may submit bids to trade excess solar electricity. Offers from both buyers and sellers consist of bid/ask prices as well as quantity in kWh. These bid/ask prices are determined based on the proposed game-theoretic approaches, where the game is non-cooperative, non-zero-sum, and simultaneous-move. All agents strategically attempt to maximize self-interests; all agents submit sealed bids without knowledge of the actions of other players. For simplicity, a two-player game is considered where an individual buyer bids against all other buyers while an individual seller bids against all other sellers. Each participant has three strategies: to bid lower, same, or higher than the previous-hour market price. The degree of bidding deviation from the previous-hour market rate is determined by each agent's low and high preferences. These two preference factors for participant $i$ (i.e., $\\delta_i^{strategy}$) are generated via sampling a normal distribution with a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 0.15.","paper":"05-double_auction","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"In this study, a double auction is of interest so that both buyers and sellers may submit bids to trade excess solar electricity. Offers from both buyers and sellers consist of bid/ask prices as well as quantity in kWh. These bid/ask prices are determined based on the proposed game-theoretic approaches, where the game is non-cooperative, non-zero-sum, and simultaneous-move. All agents strategically attempt to maximize self-interests; all agents submit sealed bids without knowledge of the actions of other players. For simplicity, a two-player game is considered where an individual buyer bids against all other buyers while an individual seller bids against all other sellers. Each participant has three strategies: to bid lower, same, or higher than the previous-hour market price. The degree of bidding deviation from the previous-hour market rate is determined by each agent's low and high preferences. These two preference factors for participant $i$ (i.e., $\\delta_i^{strategy}$) are generated via sampling a normal distribution with a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 0.15."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-18b","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1","anchor_text":"0 otherwise (5) y: Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower3) than all ","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower than all other sellers in order to win a non-zero payoff. In other words, a seller with a lower ask price gets to clear its bid before sellers with higher ask prices. In contrast, the collective of “all other sellers” may bid equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. Because the collective has a greater number of sellers, it is assumed that they have a higher possibility of winning the bid than the individual seller. When participant i is a buyer, their payoff can be determined by: $\\pi_i^{strategy} = U_i^{strategy}$ if $\\chi_i^{strategy} > \\chi_{others}^{strategy}$, $0$ otherwise (6); $\\pi_{others}^{strategy} = U_{others}^{strategy}$ if $\\chi_{others}^{strategy} \\geq \\chi_i^{strategy}$, $0$ otherwise (7). In the same way, an individual buyer must bid higher than all other buyers in order to win a non-zero payoff; the collective of “all other buyers” must bid at least equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. Such describes a “winner-takes-all” situation because the losing party gets a zero payoff.","paper":"05-double_auction","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower than all other sellers in order to win a non-zero payoff. In other words, a seller with a lower ask price gets to clear its bid before sellers with higher ask prices. In contrast, the collective of “all other sellers” may bid equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. Because the collective has a greater number of sellers, it is assumed that they have a higher possibility of winning the bid than the individual seller. When participant i is a buyer, their payoff can be determined by: $\\pi_i^{strategy} = U_i^{strategy}$ if $\\chi_i^{strategy} > \\chi_{others}^{strategy}$, $0$ otherwise (6); $\\pi_{others}^{strategy} = U_{others}^{strategy}$ if $\\chi_{others}^{strategy} \\geq \\chi_i^{strategy}$, $0$ otherwise (7). In the same way, an individual buyer must bid higher than all other buyers in order to win a non-zero payoff; the collective of “all other buyers” must bid at least equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. Such describes a “winner-takes-all” situation because the losing party gets a zero payoff."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1","anchor_text":"0 otherwise (5) y: Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower3) than all ","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower than all other sellers in order to win a non-zero payoff. In other words, a seller with a lower ask price gets to clear its bid before sellers with higher ask prices. In contrast, the collective of “all other sellers” may bid equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. ... In the same way, an individual buyer must bid higher than all other buyers in order to win a non-zero payoff; the collective of “all other buyers” must bid at least equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. ... Offer prices from all participants are sorted by price according to the natural ordering rule.","paper":"05-double_auction","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower than all other sellers in order to win a non-zero payoff. In other words, a seller with a lower ask price gets to clear its bid before sellers with higher ask prices. In contrast, the collective of “all other sellers” may bid equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. ... In the same way, an individual buyer must bid higher than all other buyers in order to win a non-zero payoff; the collective of “all other buyers” must bid at least equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. ... Offer prices from all participants are sorted by price according to the natural ordering rule."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"g pp y g ensured without the traditional grid. The BMG’s market mechanism is implemented on the Tran","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Any prosumer $P_g$ and consumer $C_a$ willing to trade electricity in the next time slot, can submit buy or sell orders via their EMTS (C6) to the market. Any order includes an order quantity and price. The market mechanism (C4) is a closed order book with a time discrete double auction in 15 min time slots. For now, consumers constantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable energy). Prosumers bid the minimum price limit that they request for selling their generation on the microgrid market.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Any prosumer $P_g$ and consumer $C_a$ willing to trade electricity in the next time slot, can submit buy or sell orders via their EMTS (C6) to the market. Any order includes an order quantity and price. The market mechanism (C4) is a closed order book with a time discrete double auction in 15 min time slots. For now, consumers constantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable energy). Prosumers bid the minimum price limit that they request for selling their generation on the microgrid market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Similar to a merit-order dispatch, the highest bidder is allocated first, then the lower bidders are allocated. The last allocated bid price represents the market clearing price for this time slot (C5). Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price will be supplied by additional energy sources (i.e. hyper-local energy or traditional “brown” energy) allocated over a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. This is well in line with the market mechanism presented by Ilic et al. [26].","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Similar to a merit-order dispatch, the highest bidder is allocated first, then the lower bidders are allocated. The last allocated bid price represents the market clearing price for this time slot (C5). Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price will be supplied by additional energy sources (i.e. hyper-local energy or traditional “brown” energy) allocated over a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. This is well in line with the market mechanism presented by Ilic et al. [26]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e35","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-matching","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Project overview","anchor_text":"g remaining power. The physical microgrid currently comprises ens 10-by-10 housing blocks and will b","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Buy and ask orders are created according to this information, and orders are send to the market mechanism (C4) that is sustained by a smart contract. Once the matching is completed, payment is carried out and a new block (here Block iv) is added to the blockchain. This block includes all current market information. The required information (i.e. conducted transactions) is also send to the involved agents via their blockchain accounts.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Buy and ask orders are created according to this information, and orders are send to the market mechanism (C4) that is sustained by a smart contract. Once the matching is completed, payment is carried out and a new block (here Block iv) is added to the blockchain. This block includes all current market information. The required information (i.e. conducted transactions) is also send to the involved agents via their blockchain accounts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For example, consumer $C_1$ bids a maximum price $x_1$ for quantity $y_1$, prosumer $P_{24}$ offers quantity $y_{24}$ for a minimum price of $x_{24}$.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"For example, consumer $C_1$ bids a maximum price $x_1$ for quantity $y_1$, prosumer $P_{24}$ offers quantity $y_{24}$ for a minimum price of $x_{24}$."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"ers in the order book would be matched. The transaction price will be determined by the sufﬁciently ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The blockchain’s verification mechanism depends on the microgrid’s setup (C1). If only trusted community members participate in the market, an identity-based consensus mechanism can be sufficiently secure. Identity-based consensus mechanisms are hash-based user authentication mechanisms that rely on every agent having a single identity that is confirmed. It is assumed that no agent can register additional identities. Thus, instead of using computational costly consensus mechanisms relying on complex cryptographic problems to prevent the dissemination of corrupted information, identity mechanisms use the simple, although hash-based, verification of the agent’s identity to prevent corrupted agents from entering the system [39]. In a microgrid market, identities could be verified and assigned by a centralized entity (e.g. government) before providing agents with the required market access. Re-verification by the consensus mechanism then relies on the assigned identities.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"The blockchain’s verification mechanism depends on the microgrid’s setup (C1). If only trusted community members participate in the market, an identity-based consensus mechanism can be sufficiently secure. Identity-based consensus mechanisms are hash-based user authentication mechanisms that rely on every agent having a single identity that is confirmed. It is assumed that no agent can register additional identities. Thus, instead of using computational costly consensus mechanisms relying on complex cryptographic problems to prevent the dissemination of corrupted information, identity mechanisms use the simple, although hash-based, verification of the agent’s identity to prevent corrupted agents from entering the system [39]. In a microgrid market, identities could be verified and assigned by a centralized entity (e.g. government) before providing agents with the required market access. Re-verification by the consensus mechanism then relies on the assigned identities."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-matching","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"( ) The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employable bidding language and a clearly defined bidding format [50]. The market mechanism is implemented by the information system (C3). Its main objective is to provide an efficient allocation of the traded energy by matching the market participants’ buy and sell orders [31]. This should best be done in (near) real time granularity [31].","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employable bidding language and a clearly defined bidding format [50]. The market mechanism is implemented by the information system (C3). Its main objective is to provide an efficient allocation of the traded energy by matching the market participants’ buy and sell orders [31]. This should best be done in (near) real time granularity [31]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"( ) The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Furthermore, constraints should exist for the minimum and maximum allocation of energy. These are especially caused by generation constraints of the microgrids’ prosumers. Ideally, different market time horizons need to be implemented to cover the various stages of electricity markets (e.g. day-ahead, intraday). The allocation mechanisms must be specifically tailored to result in an efficient allocation for every stage. We focus on intraday trading and, thus, on designing a market mechanism for this market stage concerning the actual fulfillment of energy demand, as it is the prominent stage discussed in the case study in Section 4.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"Furthermore, constraints should exist for the minimum and maximum allocation of energy. These are especially caused by generation constraints of the microgrids’ prosumers. Ideally, different market time horizons need to be implemented to cover the various stages of electricity markets (e.g. day-ahead, intraday). The allocation mechanisms must be specifically tailored to result in an efficient allocation for every stage. We focus on intraday trading and, thus, on designing a market mechanism for this market stage concerning the actual fulfillment of energy demand, as it is the prominent stage discussed in the case study in Section 4."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Evaluation and discussion","anchor_text":"y p Nevertheless, the implementation of the further market compo- nents, i.e. C4, C5, and C6, is onl","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The double auction market mechanism (C4) is cleared at discrete time intervals at uniform clearing prices. This allocation and the pricing mechanism (C5) are already implemented. Hence, C4 and C5 are sufﬁciently addressed for operation.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"The double auction market mechanism (C4) is cleared at discrete time intervals at uniform clearing prices. This allocation and the pricing mechanism (C5) are already implemented. Hence, C4 and C5 are sufﬁciently addressed for operation."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"g pp y g ensured without the traditional grid. The BMG’s market mechanism is implemented on the Tran","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Any prosumer $P_g$ and consumer $C_a$ willing to trade electricity in the next time slot, can submit buy or sell orders via their EMTS (C6) to the market. Any order includes an order quantity and price. The market mechanism (C4) is a closed order book with a time discrete double auction in 15 min time slots. For now, consumers constantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable energy). Prosumers bid the minimum price limit that they request for selling their generation on the microgrid market.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"Any prosumer $P_g$ and consumer $C_a$ willing to trade electricity in the next time slot, can submit buy or sell orders via their EMTS (C6) to the market. Any order includes an order quantity and price. The market mechanism (C4) is a closed order book with a time discrete double auction in 15 min time slots. For now, consumers constantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable energy). Prosumers bid the minimum price limit that they request for selling their generation on the microgrid market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Similar to a merit-order dispatch, the highest bidder is allocated first, then the lower bidders are allocated. The last allocated bid price represents the market clearing price for this time slot (C5). Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price will be supplied by additional energy sources (i.e. hyper-local energy or traditional “brown” energy) allocated over a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. This is well in line with the market mechanism presented by Ilic et al. [26].","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"Similar to a merit-order dispatch, the highest bidder is allocated first, then the lower bidders are allocated. The last allocated bid price represents the market clearing price for this time slot (C5). Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price will be supplied by additional energy sources (i.e. hyper-local energy or traditional “brown” energy) allocated over a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. This is well in line with the market mechanism presented by Ilic et al. [26]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Evaluation and discussion","anchor_text":"y p Nevertheless, the implementation of the further market compo- nents, i.e. C4, C5, and C6, is onl","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The double auction market mechanism (C4) is cleared at discrete time intervals at uniform clearing prices. This allocation and the pricing mechanism (C5) are already implemented. Hence, C4 and C5 are sufﬁciently addressed for operation.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"The double auction market mechanism (C4) is cleared at discrete time intervals at uniform clearing prices. This allocation and the pricing mechanism (C5) are already implemented. Hence, C4 and C5 are sufﬁciently addressed for operation."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e009","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Evaluation and discussion","anchor_text":"y p Nevertheless, the implementation of the further market compo- nents, i.e. C4, C5, and C6, is onl","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The double auction market mechanism (C4) is cleared at discrete time intervals at uniform clearing prices. This allocation and the pricing mechanism (C5) are already implemented.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"The double auction market mechanism (C4) is cleared at discrete time intervals at uniform clearing prices. This allocation and the pricing mechanism (C5) are already implemented."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"Fig. 5. High-level topo currently implemented market mechanism, the BMG will try out further market ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Financial transactions are carried out between the allocated market participants according to predefined payment rules that are also part of the market mechanism (C4). On the virtual layer, local trading is now realized and the respective funds are transferred. Yet, considered from the physical layer, consumers just pay their producing neighbors for feeding-in renewable generation into the distribution grid. These, thereby, increase the share of locally produced renewable energy in the community.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","quote":"Financial transactions are carried out between the allocated market participants according to predefined payment rules that are also part of the market mechanism (C4). On the virtual layer, local trading is now realized and the respective funds are transferred. Yet, considered from the physical layer, consumers just pay their producing neighbors for feeding-in renewable generation into the distribution grid. These, thereby, increase the share of locally produced renewable energy in the community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e022","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"Fig. 5. High-level topo currently implemented market mechanism, the BMG will try out further market ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Financial transactions are carried out between the allocated market participants according to predefined payment rules that are also part of the market mechanism (C4). On the virtual layer, local trading is now realized and the respective funds are transferred. Yet, considered from the physical layer, consumers just pay their producing neighbors for feeding-in renewable generation into the distribution grid. These, thereby, increase the share of locally produced renewable energy in the community.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","quote":"Financial transactions are carried out between the allocated market participants according to predefined payment rules that are also part of the market mechanism (C4). On the virtual layer, local trading is now realized and the respective funds are transferred. Yet, considered from the physical layer, consumers just pay their producing neighbors for feeding-in renewable generation into the distribution grid. These, thereby, increase the share of locally produced renewable energy in the community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For example, consumer $C_1$ bids a maximum price $x_1$ for quantity $y_1$, prosumer $P_{24}$ offers quantity $y_{24}$ for a minimum price of $x_{24}$.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","quote":"For example, consumer $C_1$ bids a maximum price $x_1$ for quantity $y_1$, prosumer $P_{24}$ offers quantity $y_{24}$ for a minimum price of $x_{24}$."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c1r2__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"Fig. 6","anchor_text":"Fig. 6. A schematic representation of local electricity transactions conducted in the BM employment ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"C4: Payment rules: $C_1 \\xrightarrow[\\min\\{y_1,y_{24}\\}]{x_{\\text{clearing}}} P_{24}$; $C_5 \\xrightarrow[\\min\\{y_5,y_{22}\\}]{x_{\\text{clearing}}} P_{22}$.","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","quote":"C4: Payment rules: $C_1 \\xrightarrow[\\min\\{y_1,y_{24}\\}]{x_{\\text{clearing}}} P_{24}$; $C_5 \\xrightarrow[\\min\\{y_5,y_{22}\\}]{x_{\\text{clearing}}} P_{22}$."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e021","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c1r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"Fig. 6","anchor_text":"Fig. 6. A schematic representation of local electricity transactions conducted in the BM employment ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Sell Orders: Price (MU/kWh), Quantity (kWh); Buy Order: Price (MU/kWh), Quantity (kWh); x_clearing","paper":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","quote":"Sell Orders: Price (MU/kWh), Quantity (kWh); Buy Order: Price (MU/kWh), Quantity (kWh); x_clearing"}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["capped_merit_order_allocation","merit_order_allocation_rule"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L5-3dfea6868e","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L5","ontological_kind":"capped_merit_order_allocation"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"Determine the allocation order according to the role-related price advantage of orders on the same side, and allocate the declared electricity quantity according to this priority relationship.","object":"The allocation order and transacted electricity quantity of energy buy and sell orders, retaining the different price roles of buyers and sellers.","inputs":"Order quotations, declared electricity quantities, trading roles, and the quantity conditions actually specified by the source.","operation":"On the trading side for which the source explicitly specifies ordering, the one with the stronger price competitive advantage is allocated first: buyers with higher payment quotations have priority, and sellers with lower asking prices have priority; electricity quantity is allocated according to this order.","outputs":"The allocated participants and transacted electricity quantities constrained by the price priority relationship; the specific quantity calculation rules are retained by the members.","boundary":"Limited to L5/DA allocation; it is not required that every member simultaneously specify two-sided ordering, equal-price handling, or a complete quantity algorithm; price compatibility checks, pricing, and payment cannot replace an explicit allocation priority relationship."},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"05-double_auction::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","paper_id":"05-double_auction","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"A buyer's requested energy quantity limits its cleared allocation: cleared kWh cannot exceed requested kWh. Scope: Per-buyer energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market. A seller's offered energy quantity limits its cleared allocation: cleared kWh cannot exceed supplied kWh. Scope: Per-seller energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market. A seller with a lower ask price clears its bid before sellers with higher ask prices. Scope: Seller-side clearing priority under the natural ordering rule in the studied discriminatory k-DA. Condition: When comparing sellers with different ask prices.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Per-buyer energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market. | Per-seller energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market. | Seller-side clearing priority under the natural ordering rule in the studied discriminatory k-DA.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12","v141-b-g01-ev-13","v141-b-g01-ev-18b","v141-b-g02-E07"]},"constraint_refs":["05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"850d886badc26673e6b7a831cc3bc57a6da0fb870f80bb116a79f99fe8b77d18"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"A buyer's requested energy quantity limits its cleared allocation: cleared kWh cannot exceed requested kWh. Scope: Per-buyer energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market.","expression":"\\forall i \\in \\{1, \\dots, N_B\\}:\\; B_i^{Qfill} \\le B_i^{Q}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003.var.buyer_cleared_quantity"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003.par.buyer_demand_quantity","v141-c-R003.par.num_buyers"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12","v141-b-g01-ev-13"],"ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-12","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2","anchor_text":"j re BiQfill Cleared kWh quantity for buyer i ; ≤BiQ Qfill Qm Sj Cleared kWh quantity for seller j ;","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 3","image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Where, $N_B$, $N_S$, $N_T$ Total number of buyers, sellers, and trades; $B_i^Q$ kWh quantity demanded by buyer i; $S_j^Q$ kWh quantity supplied by seller j; $B_i^{Qfill}$ Cleared kWh quantity for buyer i; $\\leq B_i^Q$; $S_j^{Qfill}$ Cleared kWh quantity for seller j; $\\leq S_j^Q$; $T_m^Q$ Total kWh quantity transacted for trade m."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-13","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"3","anchor_text":"u ke In this study, a double auction is of interest so that both buyers and us sellers may submit bi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this study, a double auction is of interest so that both buyers and sellers may submit bids to trade excess solar electricity. Offers from both buyers and sellers consist of bid/ask prices as well as quantity in kWh. These bid/ask prices are determined based on the proposed game-theoretic approaches, where the game is non-cooperative, non-zero-sum, and simultaneous-move. All agents strategically attempt to maximize self-interests; all agents submit sealed bids without knowledge of the actions of other players. For simplicity, a two-player game is considered where an individual buyer bids against all other buyers while an individual seller bids against all other sellers. Each participant has three strategies: to bid lower, same, or higher than the previous-hour market price. The degree of bidding deviation from the previous-hour market rate is determined by each agent's low and high preferences. These two preference factors for participant $i$ (i.e., $\\delta_i^{strategy}$) are generated via sampling a normal distribution with a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 0.15."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"A seller's offered energy quantity limits its cleared allocation: cleared kWh cannot exceed supplied kWh. Scope: Per-seller energy allocations in the studied local P2P solar market.","expression":"\\forall j \\in \\{1, \\dots, N_S\\}:\\; S_j^{Qfill} \\le S_j^{Q}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003.var.seller_cleared_quantity"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003.par.seller_supply_quantity","v141-c-R003.par.num_sellers"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12","v141-b-g01-ev-13"],"ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-12","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2","anchor_text":"j re BiQfill Cleared kWh quantity for buyer i ; ≤BiQ Qfill Qm Sj Cleared kWh quantity for seller j ;","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 3","image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Where, $N_B$, $N_S$, $N_T$ Total number of buyers, sellers, and trades; $B_i^Q$ kWh quantity demanded by buyer i; $S_j^Q$ kWh quantity supplied by seller j; $B_i^{Qfill}$ Cleared kWh quantity for buyer i; $\\leq B_i^Q$; $S_j^{Qfill}$ Cleared kWh quantity for seller j; $\\leq S_j^Q$; $T_m^Q$ Total kWh quantity transacted for trade m."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-13","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p03-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"3","anchor_text":"u ke In this study, a double auction is of interest so that both buyers and us sellers may submit bi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this study, a double auction is of interest so that both buyers and sellers may submit bids to trade excess solar electricity. Offers from both buyers and sellers consist of bid/ask prices as well as quantity in kWh. These bid/ask prices are determined based on the proposed game-theoretic approaches, where the game is non-cooperative, non-zero-sum, and simultaneous-move. All agents strategically attempt to maximize self-interests; all agents submit sealed bids without knowledge of the actions of other players. For simplicity, a two-player game is considered where an individual buyer bids against all other buyers while an individual seller bids against all other sellers. Each participant has three strategies: to bid lower, same, or higher than the previous-hour market price. The degree of bidding deviation from the previous-hour market rate is determined by each agent's low and high preferences. These two preference factors for participant $i$ (i.e., $\\delta_i^{strategy}$) are generated via sampling a normal distribution with a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 0.15."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A seller with a lower ask price clears its bid before sellers with higher ask prices. Scope: Seller-side clearing priority under the natural ordering rule in the studied discriminatory k-DA. Condition: When comparing sellers with different ask prices.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-18b","v141-b-g02-E07"],"ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-18b","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1","anchor_text":"0 otherwise (5) y: Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower3) than all ","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower than all other sellers in order to win a non-zero payoff. In other words, a seller with a lower ask price gets to clear its bid before sellers with higher ask prices. In contrast, the collective of “all other sellers” may bid equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. Because the collective has a greater number of sellers, it is assumed that they have a higher possibility of winning the bid than the individual seller. When participant i is a buyer, their payoff can be determined by: $\\pi_i^{strategy} = U_i^{strategy}$ if $\\chi_i^{strategy} > \\chi_{others}^{strategy}$, $0$ otherwise (6); $\\pi_{others}^{strategy} = U_{others}^{strategy}$ if $\\chi_{others}^{strategy} \\geq \\chi_i^{strategy}$, $0$ otherwise (7). In the same way, an individual buyer must bid higher than all other buyers in order to win a non-zero payoff; the collective of “all other buyers” must bid at least equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. Such describes a “winner-takes-all” situation because the losing party gets a zero payoff."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1","anchor_text":"0 otherwise (5) y: Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower3) than all ","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Due to the natural ordering rule, an individual seller must bid lower than all other sellers in order to win a non-zero payoff. In other words, a seller with a lower ask price gets to clear its bid before sellers with higher ask prices. In contrast, the collective of “all other sellers” may bid equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. ... In the same way, an individual buyer must bid higher than all other buyers in order to win a non-zero payoff; the collective of “all other buyers” must bid at least equal to participant i to win a non-zero payoff. ... Offer prices from all participants are sorted by price according to the natural ordering rule."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003.var.buyer_cleared_quantity","symbol":"B_i^{Qfill}","meaning":"Cleared kWh allocation for buyer i as produced by clearing.local.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative energy quantity in kWh (cleared buyer allocation), bounded above by B_i^Q","unit":"kWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003.var.buyer_cleared_quantity"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003.var.seller_cleared_quantity","symbol":"S_j^{Qfill}","meaning":"Cleared kWh allocation for seller j as produced by clearing.local.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative energy quantity in kWh (cleared seller allocation), bounded above by S_j^Q","unit":"kWh","indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003.var.seller_cleared_quantity"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003.par.buyer_demand_quantity","symbol":"B_i^Q","meaning":"kWh quantity demanded by buyer i; the fixed per-buyer requested energy that limits the buyer's cleared allocation.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12","v141-b-g01-ev-13"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003.par.buyer_demand_quantity"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003.par.seller_supply_quantity","symbol":"S_j^Q","meaning":"kWh quantity supplied by seller j; the fixed per-seller offered energy that limits the seller's cleared allocation.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kWh","indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003.par.seller_supply_quantity"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003.par.num_buyers","symbol":"N_B","meaning":"Total number of buyers; cardinality of the per-buyer index set used in constraint C001.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003.par.num_buyers"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003.par.num_sellers","symbol":"N_S","meaning":"Total number of sellers; cardinality of the per-seller index set used in constraint C002.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-12"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003.par.num_sellers"}]},{"unit_id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","paper_id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"Submitted buy orders provide requested quantities and bid prices to local clearing, which allocates the highest bidder first and then lower bidders. Scope: BMG specified local double-auction mechanism Submitted sell orders provide offered quantities and prices to the market mechanism responsible for local energy allocation. Scope: BMG specified local double-auction mechanism Matching participants' buy and sell orders is specified as the means of achieving efficient local energy allocation. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4 Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price receive additional energy allocated through a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. Scope: BMG allocation of additional hyper-local or traditional energy sources to consumers Condition: Consumers do not undercut the clearing price. Minimum and maximum energy-allocation constraints should exist, especially to account for prosumer generation constraints. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4 Different market time horizons should ideally be implemented, and allocation mechanisms must be tailored to achieve efficient allocation at each stage. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design across electricity-market time horizons Local energy allocation should preferably operate at near-real-time granularity. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4 Local clearing uses a closed order book and a time-discrete double auction in 15-minute slots. Scope: BMG specified double-auction mechanism","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"BMG allocation of additional hyper-local or traditional energy sources to consumers | BMG specified double-auction mechanism | BMG specified local double-auction mechanism | General microgrid market-mechanism design across electricity-market time horizons | General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e35"]},"constraint_refs":["24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C007","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C008"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"1bbe8c3fe342874c6a3359fb3aba730cc3386f72459200ac03dd4f4c78b1f84b"},{"id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R010::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","market_meaning":"Local allocation determines the price-setting last allocated bid, whose price becomes the uniform market clearing price for that time slot. Scope: BMG closed-order-book double-auction design with 15 min time slots. Condition: When the described double-auction mechanism is used. A clearing price is determined in every time slot; prices vary with demand, supply and socio-economic characteristics. Scope: BMG stated time-slot pricing design. Condition: When the described time-slot pricing design is used. Energy surplus should lower the microgrid energy price. Scope: General microgrid-market pricing recommendations in the C1–C7 framework. Condition: There is an energy surplus. A lack of local energy increases the market price. Scope: General microgrid-market pricing discussion in the C1–C7 framework. Condition: Local energy is lacking. The local energy price may exceed the external grid price. Scope: General microgrid-market pricing discussion in the C1–C7 framework. Condition: Socio-economic reasons, such as supporting local renewable energy, are considered. The electricity price was predetermined and fixed at the traditional price for renewable energy in Brooklyn. Scope: BMG reported three month trial with one consumer and one prosumer. Condition: During this trial, in which the market mechanism was not actively used. Each transaction may have an individual transaction price. Scope: BMG proposed further market-mechanism trials, before selection of the final mechanism. Condition: If the proposed pay-as-you bid order book is used.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"BMG closed-order-book double-auction design with 15 min time slots. | BMG proposed further market-mechanism trials, before selection of the final mechanism. | BMG reported three month trial with one consumer and one prosumer. | BMG stated time-slot pricing design. | General microgrid-market pricing discussion in the C1–C7 framework. | General microgrid-market pricing recommendations in the C1–C7 framework.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e31","v141-b-g03-e010","v141-b-g03-e016"]},"constraint_refs":["24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C001"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"e5dc359e0f8848970cafe680480a92663cbc24c7dcee3b49ef9157fafdfb1042"},{"id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","market_meaning":"Local allocation supplies the allocated participants between whom financial transactions are carried out according to predefined payment rules. Scope: BMG virtual-market financial transactions between allocated participants. The local transaction price determined by the pricing mechanism is used for monetary payment; the illustrated BMG payment rules use the clearing price. Scope: Schematic local order-book transactions and the illustrated BMG payment rules. Payment is carried out once counterparty matching is completed. Scope: BMG smart-contract market transaction workflow. Condition: Matching has been completed. Consumers pay their producing neighbors for feeding renewable generation into the distribution grid. Scope: Physical-layer interpretation of BMG virtual-market payments. Condition: Producing neighbors feed renewable generation into the distribution grid.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"BMG smart-contract market transaction workflow. | BMG virtual-market financial transactions between allocated participants. | Physical-layer interpretation of BMG virtual-market payments. | Schematic local order-book transactions and the illustrated BMG payment rules.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g02-e30","v141-b-g02-e35","v141-b-g03-e022"]},"constraint_refs":["24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C005"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"cbcdd5e0a18793905b4452d98d282abe6114cf83dee8226cbc4b4d7be776a3c1"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Submitted buy orders provide requested quantities and bid prices to local clearing, which allocates the highest bidder first and then lower bidders. Scope: BMG specified local double-auction mechanism","expression":"B=\\{(x_i,y_i)\\}_{i\\in I_B};\\quad \\forall i,j\\in I_B:\\ x_i>x_j\\Rightarrow i\\prec_B j\\ \\text{(i is allocated before j in local clearing)}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-x-order-price","v141-c-R004-y-order-quantity"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e35","v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e21"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"g pp y g ensured without the traditional grid. The BMG’s market mechanism is implemented on the Tran","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Any prosumer $P_g$ and consumer $C_a$ willing to trade electricity in the next time slot, can submit buy or sell orders via their EMTS (C6) to the market. Any order includes an order quantity and price. The market mechanism (C4) is a closed order book with a time discrete double auction in 15 min time slots. For now, consumers constantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable energy). Prosumers bid the minimum price limit that they request for selling their generation on the microgrid market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Similar to a merit-order dispatch, the highest bidder is allocated first, then the lower bidders are allocated. The last allocated bid price represents the market clearing price for this time slot (C5). Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price will be supplied by additional energy sources (i.e. hyper-local energy or traditional “brown” energy) allocated over a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. This is well in line with the market mechanism presented by Ilic et al. [26]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e35","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-matching","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Project overview","anchor_text":"g remaining power. The physical microgrid currently comprises ens 10-by-10 housing blocks and will b","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Buy and ask orders are created according to this information, and orders are send to the market mechanism (C4) that is sustained by a smart contract. Once the matching is completed, payment is carried out and a new block (here Block iv) is added to the blockchain. This block includes all current market information. The required information (i.e. conducted transactions) is also send to the involved agents via their blockchain accounts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For example, consumer $C_1$ bids a maximum price $x_1$ for quantity $y_1$, prosumer $P_{24}$ offers quantity $y_{24}$ for a minimum price of $x_{24}$."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"ers in the order book would be matched. The transaction price will be determined by the sufﬁciently ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The blockchain’s verification mechanism depends on the microgrid’s setup (C1). If only trusted community members participate in the market, an identity-based consensus mechanism can be sufficiently secure. Identity-based consensus mechanisms are hash-based user authentication mechanisms that rely on every agent having a single identity that is confirmed. It is assumed that no agent can register additional identities. Thus, instead of using computational costly consensus mechanisms relying on complex cryptographic problems to prevent the dissemination of corrupted information, identity mechanisms use the simple, although hash-based, verification of the agent’s identity to prevent corrupted agents from entering the system [39]. In a microgrid market, identities could be verified and assigned by a centralized entity (e.g. government) before providing agents with the required market access. Re-verification by the consensus mechanism then relies on the assigned identities."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Submitted sell orders provide offered quantities and prices to the market mechanism responsible for local energy allocation. Scope: BMG specified local double-auction mechanism","expression":"S=\\{(x_i,y_i)\\}_{i\\in I_S}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-x-order-price","v141-c-R004-y-order-quantity"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e35","v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e21"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"g pp y g ensured without the traditional grid. The BMG’s market mechanism is implemented on the Tran","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Any prosumer $P_g$ and consumer $C_a$ willing to trade electricity in the next time slot, can submit buy or sell orders via their EMTS (C6) to the market. Any order includes an order quantity and price. The market mechanism (C4) is a closed order book with a time discrete double auction in 15 min time slots. For now, consumers constantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable energy). Prosumers bid the minimum price limit that they request for selling their generation on the microgrid market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-matching","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"( ) The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employable bidding language and a clearly defined bidding format [50]. The market mechanism is implemented by the information system (C3). Its main objective is to provide an efficient allocation of the traded energy by matching the market participants’ buy and sell orders [31]. This should best be done in (near) real time granularity [31]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e35","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-matching","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1 Project overview","anchor_text":"g remaining power. The physical microgrid currently comprises ens 10-by-10 housing blocks and will b","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Buy and ask orders are created according to this information, and orders are send to the market mechanism (C4) that is sustained by a smart contract. Once the matching is completed, payment is carried out and a new block (here Block iv) is added to the blockchain. This block includes all current market information. The required information (i.e. conducted transactions) is also send to the involved agents via their blockchain accounts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For example, consumer $C_1$ bids a maximum price $x_1$ for quantity $y_1$, prosumer $P_{24}$ offers quantity $y_{24}$ for a minimum price of $x_{24}$."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"ers in the order book would be matched. The transaction price will be determined by the sufﬁciently ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The blockchain’s verification mechanism depends on the microgrid’s setup (C1). If only trusted community members participate in the market, an identity-based consensus mechanism can be sufficiently secure. Identity-based consensus mechanisms are hash-based user authentication mechanisms that rely on every agent having a single identity that is confirmed. It is assumed that no agent can register additional identities. Thus, instead of using computational costly consensus mechanisms relying on complex cryptographic problems to prevent the dissemination of corrupted information, identity mechanisms use the simple, although hash-based, verification of the agent’s identity to prevent corrupted agents from entering the system [39]. In a microgrid market, identities could be verified and assigned by a centralized entity (e.g. government) before providing agents with the required market access. Re-verification by the consensus mechanism then relies on the assigned identities."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Matching participants' buy and sell orders is specified as the means of achieving efficient local energy allocation. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e09"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-matching","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"( ) The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employable bidding language and a clearly defined bidding format [50]. The market mechanism is implemented by the information system (C3). Its main objective is to provide an efficient allocation of the traded energy by matching the market participants’ buy and sell orders [31]. This should best be done in (near) real time granularity [31]."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C004","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price receive additional energy allocated through a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. Scope: BMG allocation of additional hyper-local or traditional energy sources to consumers Condition: Consumers do not undercut the clearing price.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Similar to a merit-order dispatch, the highest bidder is allocated first, then the lower bidders are allocated. The last allocated bid price represents the market clearing price for this time slot (C5). Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price will be supplied by additional energy sources (i.e. hyper-local energy or traditional “brown” energy) allocated over a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. This is well in line with the market mechanism presented by Ilic et al. [26]."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Minimum and maximum energy-allocation constraints should exist, especially to account for prosumer generation constraints. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e10"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"( ) The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Furthermore, constraints should exist for the minimum and maximum allocation of energy. These are especially caused by generation constraints of the microgrids’ prosumers. Ideally, different market time horizons need to be implemented to cover the various stages of electricity markets (e.g. day-ahead, intraday). The allocation mechanisms must be specifically tailored to result in an efficient allocation for every stage. We focus on intraday trading and, thus, on designing a market mechanism for this market stage concerning the actual fulfillment of energy demand, as it is the prominent stage discussed in the case study in Section 4."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Different market time horizons should ideally be implemented, and allocation mechanisms must be tailored to achieve efficient allocation at each stage. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design across electricity-market time horizons","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e10"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"( ) The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Furthermore, constraints should exist for the minimum and maximum allocation of energy. These are especially caused by generation constraints of the microgrids’ prosumers. Ideally, different market time horizons need to be implemented to cover the various stages of electricity markets (e.g. day-ahead, intraday). The allocation mechanisms must be specifically tailored to result in an efficient allocation for every stage. We focus on intraday trading and, thus, on designing a market mechanism for this market stage concerning the actual fulfillment of energy demand, as it is the prominent stage discussed in the case study in Section 4."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C007","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local energy allocation should preferably operate at near-real-time granularity. Scope: General microgrid market-mechanism design, component C4","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e09"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-matching","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Market mechanism (C4)","anchor_text":"( ) The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The market mechanism comprises the market’s allocation and payment rules, and provides an employable bidding language and a clearly defined bidding format [50]. The market mechanism is implemented by the information system (C3). Its main objective is to provide an efficient allocation of the traded energy by matching the market participants’ buy and sell orders [31]. This should best be done in (near) real time granularity [31]."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C008","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local clearing uses a closed order book and a time-discrete double auction in 15-minute slots. Scope: BMG specified double-auction mechanism","expression":"\\Delta t=15\\ \\text{min}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R004-theta-slot-length"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e16"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C008","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"g pp y g ensured without the traditional grid. The BMG’s market mechanism is implemented on the Tran","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Any prosumer $P_g$ and consumer $C_a$ willing to trade electricity in the next time slot, can submit buy or sell orders via their EMTS (C6) to the market. Any order includes an order quantity and price. The market mechanism (C4) is a closed order book with a time discrete double auction in 15 min time slots. For now, consumers constantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable energy). Prosumers bid the minimum price limit that they request for selling their generation on the microgrid market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Evaluation and discussion","anchor_text":"y p Nevertheless, the implementation of the further market compo- nents, i.e. C4, C5, and C6, is onl","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The double auction market mechanism (C4) is cleared at discrete time intervals at uniform clearing prices. This allocation and the pricing mechanism (C5) are already implemented. Hence, C4 and C5 are sufﬁciently addressed for operation."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local allocation determines the price-setting last allocated bid, whose price becomes the uniform market clearing price for that time slot. Scope: BMG closed-order-book double-auction design with 15 min time slots. Condition: When the described double-auction mechanism is used.","expression":"p^{\\mathrm{clear}}_t = p^{\\mathrm{last}}_t \\quad \\text{(for the time slot } t\\text{; the resulting uniform clearing price applies to transactions in that slot)}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["x_clear_t","x_last_bid_t"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g03-e009"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"g pp y g ensured without the traditional grid. The BMG’s market mechanism is implemented on the Tran","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Any prosumer $P_g$ and consumer $C_a$ willing to trade electricity in the next time slot, can submit buy or sell orders via their EMTS (C6) to the market. Any order includes an order quantity and price. The market mechanism (C4) is a closed order book with a time discrete double auction in 15 min time slots. For now, consumers constantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable energy). Prosumers bid the minimum price limit that they request for selling their generation on the microgrid market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Similar to a merit-order dispatch, the highest bidder is allocated first, then the lower bidders are allocated. The last allocated bid price represents the market clearing price for this time slot (C5). Consumers that do not undercut the clearing price will be supplied by additional energy sources (i.e. hyper-local energy or traditional “brown” energy) allocated over a similar bidding mechanism or predefined prices. This is well in line with the market mechanism presented by Ilic et al. [26]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Evaluation and discussion","anchor_text":"y p Nevertheless, the implementation of the further market compo- nents, i.e. C4, C5, and C6, is onl","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The double auction market mechanism (C4) is cleared at discrete time intervals at uniform clearing prices. This allocation and the pricing mechanism (C5) are already implemented. Hence, C4 and C5 are sufﬁciently addressed for operation."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e009","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.3 Evaluation and discussion","anchor_text":"y p Nevertheless, the implementation of the further market compo- nents, i.e. C4, C5, and C6, is onl","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The double auction market mechanism (C4) is cleared at discrete time intervals at uniform clearing prices. This allocation and the pricing mechanism (C5) are already implemented."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R012-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local allocation supplies the allocated participants between whom financial transactions are carried out according to predefined payment rules. Scope: BMG virtual-market financial transactions between allocated participants.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g03-e022"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"Fig. 5. High-level topo currently implemented market mechanism, the BMG will try out further market ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Financial transactions are carried out between the allocated market participants according to predefined payment rules that are also part of the market mechanism (C4). On the virtual layer, local trading is now realized and the respective funds are transferred. Yet, considered from the physical layer, consumers just pay their producing neighbors for feeding-in renewable generation into the distribution grid. These, thereby, increase the share of locally produced renewable energy in the community."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e022","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p08-c1r4__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":"Fig. 5. High-level topo currently implemented market mechanism, the BMG will try out further market ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Financial transactions are carried out between the allocated market participants according to predefined payment rules that are also part of the market mechanism (C4). On the virtual layer, local trading is now realized and the respective funds are transferred. Yet, considered from the physical layer, consumers just pay their producing neighbors for feeding-in renewable generation into the distribution grid. These, thereby, increase the share of locally produced renewable energy in the community."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R012-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"In the illustrated BMG payment rules, consumer C1 is annotated as paying producer P24 and consumer C5 as paying producer P22, with the clearing price x_clearing shown above the arrow and the allocated quantity min{y_1,y_24} or min{y_5,y_22} shown below the arrow. The source presents this arrow annotation rather than a separately written payment-amount equation.","expression":"C_1 \\xrightarrow[\\min\\{y_1,y_{24}\\}]{x_{\\text{clearing}}} P_{24};\\quad C_5 \\xrightarrow[\\min\\{y_5,y_{22}\\}]{x_{\\text{clearing}}} P_{22}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","matching.counterparties","pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta_x_clearing","theta_y_consumer","theta_y_producer"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e021"],"ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2 Market mechanism","anchor_text":", stantly bid their maximum price limit for their preferred energy sources (e.g. local renewable ene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For example, consumer $C_1$ bids a maximum price $x_1$ for quantity $y_1$, prosumer $P_{24}$ offers quantity $y_{24}$ for a minimum price of $x_{24}$."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c1r2__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"Fig. 6","anchor_text":"Fig. 6. A schematic representation of local electricity transactions conducted in the BM employment ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"C4: Payment rules: $C_1 \\xrightarrow[\\min\\{y_1,y_{24}\\}]{x_{\\text{clearing}}} P_{24}$; $C_5 \\xrightarrow[\\min\\{y_5,y_{22}\\}]{x_{\\text{clearing}}} P_{22}$."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e021","location":{"location_id":"src-d1d75d8eed-p09-c1r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"Fig. 6","anchor_text":"Fig. 6. A schematic representation of local electricity transactions conducted in the BM employment ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 6","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Sell Orders: Price (MU/kWh), Quantity (kWh); Buy Order: Price (MU/kWh), Quantity (kWh); x_clearing"}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R004-x-order-price","symbol":"x_i","meaning":"Price limit of submitted order i; maximum price for consumer buy orders and minimum price for prosumer sell orders, as in the source examples x_1 and x_24.","kind":"operational","domain":"Submitted buy or sell orders indexed by i; price in MU/kWh; source gives numeric examples but no explicit numeric bound or complete index set.","unit":"MU/kWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::x::v141-c-R004-x-order-price"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R004-y-order-quantity","symbol":"y_i","meaning":"Energy quantity of submitted order i; requested quantity for buy orders and offered quantity for sell orders, as in the source examples y_1 and y_24.","kind":"operational","domain":"Submitted buy or sell orders indexed by i; quantity in kWh; source gives numeric examples but no explicit numeric bound or complete index set.","unit":"kWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::x::v141-c-R004-y-order-quantity"},{"variable_id":"x_clear_t","symbol":"p^{\\mathrm{clear}}_t","meaning":"Uniform market clearing price for time slot t; the source identifies this price with the last allocated bid price and says double-auction clearing occurs at uniform prices.","kind":"operational","domain":"Local time-slot price; the source does not state a numeric range in the supplied rule evidence.","unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g03-e009","v141-b-g03-e010"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","declaration_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R010::x::x_clear_t"},{"variable_id":"x_last_bid_t","symbol":"p^{\\mathrm{last}}_t","meaning":"Price of the last allocated bid in time slot t, i.e. the price-setting last allocated bid.","kind":"operational","domain":"Bid price in the local order book; the source does not state a numeric range in the supplied rule evidence.","unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","declaration_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R010::x::x_last_bid_t"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-theta-slot-length","symbol":"\\Delta t","meaning":"Duration of each discrete double-auction time slot used by local clearing.","value":{"kind":"numeric","number_value":15.0,"text_value":null},"unit":"min","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","declaration_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::theta::v141-c-R004-theta-slot-length"},{"parameter_id":"theta_x_clearing","symbol":"x_{\\text{clearing}}","meaning":"Clearing price for the time slot used as the transaction price in the illustrated BMG payment-rule annotation. The quoted payment annotation does not give a separate formula for how the clearing price is determined.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"x_{\\text{clearing}}"},"unit":"MU/kWh","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e021"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::theta::theta_x_clearing"},{"parameter_id":"theta_y_consumer","symbol":"y_i","meaning":"Order quantity submitted by a consumer i, used in the payment annotation's allocated quantity min{y_i,y_j}; the source presents y_1 and y_5 as instances.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"y_i"},"unit":"kWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e021"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::theta::theta_y_consumer"},{"parameter_id":"theta_y_producer","symbol":"y_j","meaning":"Order quantity offered by a prosumer j, used in the payment annotation's allocated quantity min{y_i,y_j}; the source presents y_24 and y_22 as instances.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"y_j"},"unit":"kWh","indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g03-e021"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::theta::theta_y_producer"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"05-double_auction::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","stage":"L5","subtype":"capped_merit_order_allocation","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Determine non-negative buyer and seller transacted energy quantities, satisfying ∀i: B_i^{Qfill}≤B_i^Q and ∀j: S_j^{Qfill}≤S_j^Q; sellers with different asking prices are cleared with priority to lower asking prices.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: buyer and seller transacted energy quantities","Operation: allocate according to the declared quantity upper limits and the seller price priority rule","Output: B_i^{Qfill} and S_j^{Qfill}"],"variation_fields":["The number of buyers N_B, the number of sellers N_S, and their respective declared energy quantities","The seller priority relation applies to comparisons of different asking prices","Local P2P solar energy trading scenario"],"constraint_refs":["05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003"],"paper_id":"05-double_auction","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","template_code":"DA"},{"unit_id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","stage":"L5","subtype":"merit_order_allocation_rule","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The described double auction allocates energy according to buy and sell orders, first allocating to the highest buyer bid and then to lower bids, that is, when x_i>x_j, i is allocated before j; it outputs the allocated participants and energy quantities. The two illustrated allocation quantities are min{y_1,y_24} and min{y_5,y_22}, respectively.","form_kind":"procedural","identity_fields":["Object: energy allocation corresponding to buy and sell orders","Operation: determine the allocation order in descending order of buyer bids","Output: allocated participants and transacted energy quantities"],"variation_fields":["Closed order book and 15-minute discrete time intervals","The general framework recommends setting allocation upper and lower limits and considering prosumer generation constraints","The general framework recommends near-real-time allocation and allocation designed separately according to market time horizon","Consumers whose bids are not below the clearing price can obtain additional energy through a similar auction or a preset price","The paired energy quantities shown in the figure are retained only as examples and are not extrapolated as a complete allocation algorithm for all orders"],"constraint_refs":["24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C007","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C008","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C005"],"paper_id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","template_code":"DA"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"05-double_auction::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","source_definition":"Determine non-negative buyer and seller transacted energy quantities, satisfying ∀i: B_i^{Qfill}≤B_i^Q and ∀j: S_j^{Qfill}≤S_j^Q; sellers with different asking prices are cleared with priority to lower asking prices.","retained_variants":["∀i∈{1,…,N_B}: 0≤B_i^{Qfill}≤B_i^Q; ∀j∈{1,…,N_S}: 0≤S_j^{Qfill}≤S_j^Q, with electricity quantity in kWh.","The explicit allocation priority relationship applies to sellers with different asking prices; no equal-price ordering rule is added.","The scenario is local P2P surplus solar energy trading and discriminatory k-DA.","The buyer-benefit winning conditions and the individual's strategic assumptions about other groups in the complete quotation are retained in their original context and are not extrapolated as a complete buyer allocation algorithm."],"constraint_refs":["05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","05-double_auction::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003"]},{"unit_id":"24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","source_definition":"The described double auction allocates energy according to buy and sell orders, first allocating to the highest buyer bid and then to lower bids, that is, when x_i>x_j, i is allocated before j; it outputs the allocated participants and energy quantities. The two illustrated allocation quantities are min{y_1,y_24} and min{y_5,y_22}, respectively.","retained_variants":["A closed order book and a 15-minute discrete double auction are adopted; equal-price handling is not extrapolated.","x_i are, respectively, the consumer's maximum willingness-to-pay price and the prosumer's minimum selling price, in MU/kWh; y_i are, respectively, the requested or offered energy quantity, in kWh, and no complete numerical bounds are given.","min{y_1,y_24} and min{y_5,y_22} are only illustrative examples and are not generalized as a complete allocation algorithm for all orders.","Allocation upper and lower limits, near-real-time granularity, and adaptation to different market time horizons are general framework recommendations and are not rewritten as an implemented unified quantity constraint.","The branch in which consumers whose bids are not below the clearing price obtain additional energy through a similar auction or a preset price is retained separately.","The last allocated bid is used for uniform pricing, and the allocated participants are used for payment; both are subsequent uses of the allocation result and are not included in the parent operation."],"constraint_refs":["24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C002","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C003","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C004","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C005","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C006","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C007","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C008","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C001","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","24-brooklyn_microgrid::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C005"]}],"source_parent_definition":{"definition":"Determine the allocation order according to the role-related price advantage of orders on the same side, and allocate the declared electricity quantity according to this priority relationship.","object":"The allocation order and transacted electricity quantity of energy buy and sell orders, retaining the different price roles of buyers and sellers.","inputs":"Order quotations, declared electricity quantities, trading roles, and the quantity conditions actually specified by the source.","operation":"On the trading side for which the source explicitly specifies ordering, the one with the stronger price competitive advantage is allocated first: buyers with higher payment quotations have priority, and sellers with lower asking prices have priority; electricity quantity is allocated according to this order.","outputs":"The allocated participants and transacted electricity quantities constrained by the price priority relationship; the specific quantity calculation rules are retained by the members.","boundary":"Limited to L5/DA allocation; it is not required that every member simultaneously specify two-sided ordering, equal-price handling, or a complete quantity algorithm; price compatibility checks, pricing, and payment cannot replace an explicit allocation priority relationship."},"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/260"});
